@page "/expivot/sources"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Pivot


<DocsPage Product="ExPivot" Title="Sources"
          Lead="A Pivot Source is what a report is computed from: your records in the browser, a Snapshot read from a file, or a server that answers the same questions over your own transport.">

    <DocsSection Title="How a source is asked">
        <p>
            ExPivot requests a report Window and the metadata describing its full extent. Local data is
            calculated by the same engine that a server uses. A slow answer leaves the report under its loading
            indication, and a newer question cancels the older one. Copy and Selection Summary can include rows
            outside the Window; every operation names the Report Version it answers.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong><code>DataSource</code></strong> accepts <code>PivotSource.From</code> over local records or a Snapshot, including a CSV that stays in the browser.</li>
            <li><strong><code>Source</code></strong> accepts a <code>PivotReportSource</code>. Its <code>Fetch</code> factory uses your delegates to request server report Windows and operations.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the source in a field.</strong> Each report owns independent calculation state; a new data provider initializes a new calculation.</li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Over your objects">
        <p>
            <code>PivotFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</code> declares each field once with a typed accessor
            (<code>Sales.cs</code> shows them all). The declaration makes both the Snapshot column the records
            are read into, without boxing a value, and the Pivot Field over it:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Text</strong>, with Excel's custom list (<code>itemOrder</code>) or an Order Key; <code>null</code> is the Item <code>(blank)</code>, and <code>""</code> is a value.</li>
            <li><strong>Number</strong> over <code>decimal</code> (exact), <code>int</code> or <code>long</code> (exact), or <code>double</code> (Excel's arithmetic).</li>
            <li><strong>Date</strong> over <code>DateTime</code>, <code>DateOnly</code> or <code>DateTimeOffset</code>, with <code>Year</code>, <code>Quarter</code> and <code>Month</code> as fields of their own, ordered by the calendar.</li>
            <li><strong>Boolean</strong>, labelled <code>TRUE</code> and <code>FALSE</code>; and a <strong>Key</strong>, the Record Key a Change Batch finds a record by.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            <code>PivotSource.From(records, fields)</code> reads the records on the calling thread. For a large
            list in a browser, <code>PivotSource.FromAsync</code> reads them in slices of about 30 ms, yielding
            between them and reporting progress, so the page keeps painting.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(LoadInSlices)" Mud="typeof(LoadInSlicesMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Over a Snapshot">
        <p>
            A <a href="data">Snapshot</a> is pivoted as it is, wherever it came from. <code>PivotSource.From(snapshot)</code>
            offers one field per column, captioned and typed by it; or name the fields you want over its columns,
            a date part naming the Date column it is a part of. A field naming a column the Snapshot does not
            have is refused by name.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FromSnapshot)" Mud="typeof(FromSnapshotMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="From a CSV">
        <p>
            <code>ExGrid.Data</code> reads a CSV straight into a Snapshot's columns under a <code>CsvSchema</code> you
            declare: what each column is and how to read it. Nothing is guessed, because a guess reads the account
            number <code>004417</code> as the number 4,417. A file the user brings comes from Blazor's
            <code>InputFile</code> (<code>e.File.OpenReadStream(maxAllowedSize)</code>); the Example writes one in
            memory instead.
        </p>
        <p>
            A value its column cannot read refuses the whole file, naming the row and the column. The report is
            then not drawn at all, rather than drawn over the rows that happened to read. See
            <a href="data">Snapshot</a> for the Schema's options and for suggesting one from a file nobody has
            described.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FromCsv)" Mud="typeof(FromCsvMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/SalesCsv.cs", "Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A server's source">
        <p>
            <code>PivotReportSource.Fetch</code> carries your Window, Items, Copy, Summary and Details transports.
            ExPivot ships no HTTP client or endpoint. <code>PivotReportJson</code> writes their questions and answers
            as versioned JSON, preserving decimal and double values. Keep a <code>PivotReportSource.From(data)</code>
            on the server. Its data can be a Snapshot, or a SQL-backed <code>PivotSource</code> that refreshes a full
            aggregate answer; the browser still receives only report Windows.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>UpdateMode</code> declares incremental changes or full refresh. The bundled Snapshot source is incremental.</li>
            <li>Culture, formats and display words are explicit; custom Order Keys select policies registered on the server.</li>
            <li>The Consumer owns transport and report-state disposal. A missing baseline is recovered automatically; an unavailable older operation refuses by name.</li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ServerSource)" Mud="typeof(ServerSourceMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/SalesServer.cs", "Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            The server here is simulated in the page, with half a second of latency: change the layout and watch the
            report wait under its loading indication.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Caps">
        <p>
            <code>Caps</code> (<code>PivotCaps</code>) refuses, by name, a layout whose report is too large to read:
            more than 200,000 leaves, or more rows or columns than Excel's 1,048,576 and 16,384. The report on screen
            stays, and the Pivot Toolbar says why.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="API">
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(global::ExPivot.Engine.PivotSource)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(global::ExPivot.Engine.SnapshotPivotSource)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(global::ExPivot.Engine.PivotReportSource)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(global::ExPivot.Engine.FetchingPivotReportSource)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(global::ExPivot.PivotCaps)" />
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
